[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Anti-Patterns, Code Smells, and Refactoring Legacy Code to SOLID"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Legacy systems often suffer from accumulated technical debt, resulting in fragile codebases that are difficult to maintain, test, and extend. By identifying common anti-patterns and code smells, developers can systematically apply the SOLID principles to transform monolithic, unreadable code into modular, robust software."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Identifying Common Code Smells and Anti-Patterns"},{"order":4,"type":"bullet_point","text":"God Object: A single class that knows too much or does too much, violating the Single Responsibility Principle."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Spaghetti Code: Control structures are intertwined and complex, making the execution path extremely hard to follow."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Shotgun Surgery: A single change requires small modifications in many different classes and modules."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Tight Coupling: High dependency between software modules, making unit testing and reuse nearly impossible."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Legacy Code Example (The God Object Anti-Pattern)"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"Consider the following legacy class that handles user data, database persistence, and email notifications all in one place:"},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"class UserManager {
  public void registerUser(String username, String email, String password) {
    // Validate user
    if (username == null || email == null) {
      throw new IllegalArgumentException("Invalid data");
    }

    // Hash password (business logic)
    String hashedPassword = hashPassword(password);

    // Save to Database (Data Access)
    try (Connection conn = DriverManager.getConnection("jdbc:mysql://localhost/db")) {
      PreparedStatement stmt = conn.prepareStatement("INSERT INTO users VALUES (?, ?, ?)");
      stmt.setString(1, username);
      stmt.setString(2, email);
      stmt.setString(3, hashedPassword);
      stmt.executeUpdate();
    } catch (Exception e) {
      e.printStackTrace();
    }

    // Send Email (Infrastructure)
    sendEmail(email, "Welcome to our platform!");
  }

  private String hashPassword(String password) {
    return "HASHED_" + password;
  }

  private void sendEmail(String to, String message) {
    System.out.println("Sending email to " + to + ": " + message);
  }
}"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Refactoring to SOLID Principles"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"To fix this God Object, we apply the Single Responsibility Principle (SRP) and Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP). We separate concerns into dedicated classes: a repository for data access, a service for notifications, and a domain model for user logic."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"public interface UserRepository {
  void save(User user);
}

public interface EmailService {
  void sendWelcomeEmail(String email);
}

public class SqlUserRepository implements UserRepository {
  @Override
  public void save(User user) {
    // Database insertion logic here
    System.out.println("Saving user to database: " + user.getUsername());
  }
}

public class SmtpEmailService implements EmailService {
  @Override
  public void sendWelcomeEmail(String email) {
    System.out.println("Sending SMTP email to: " + email);
  }
}

public class UserRegistrationService {
  private final UserRepository userRepository;
  private final EmailService emailService;

  public UserRegistrationService(UserRepository userRepository, EmailService emailService) {
    this.userRepository = userRepository;
    this.emailService = emailService;
  }

  public void register(String username, String email, String password) {
    User user = new User(username, email, password);
    userRepository.save(user);
    emailService.sendWelcomeEmail(email);
  }
}"},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"By breaking down coupled legacy classes into small, cohesive components backed by interfaces, you ensure your codebase remains maintainable, highly testable, and compliant with SOLID principles."}]
